{"B_expression":"-1/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))**2)","C_expression":"0","derivation":"Retained from draft. The expression reconstructs the R1 source-only quadratic-canceling coefficient. At u=0 the increment reduces to g = r/2 + r^2*B = r/2 - r^2/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))^2) = (1-sqrt(1+r))/sqrt(1+r) = sqrt(1+r)-1 (rationalized), so (1+g)^2 = 1+r exactly and the pure remainder 2g+g^2-r vanishes. Smoothness: sqrt(1+r) is C-infinity for r>-1; chi_r support is |r|<=1/2, giving 1+r>=1/2>0 throughout the evaluation rectangle. C=0 retains the published source construction; the cross term 2ug is of order ur and is not addressed here. Numerical feedback confirms: all in-support rows (|r|<=1/2, u=0) show residuals at or below 1.8e-71 (machine rounding); the single out-of-support row (r=-1, chi_r=0) correctly falls back to g=r/2 with residual 0.25, as expected. No NO_APPLIED_UPDATE failures.","inspect":[],"note":"Confirmed by first-stage numerical execution. Successor considerations: (1) After stage 1, z_after != 1 at most points, so u = z_after - 1 is nonzero. The whole residual then includes the cross term 2*u*g, which is O(ur). This is expected and documented; it does not invalidate the B-only construction but means the residual is no longer exactly zero. (2) The chi_r transition zone (1/4 < |r| < 1/2) smoothly blends B toward 0; no discontinuity. (3) If a future stage drives r below -1 before chi_r fades to zero, sqrt(1+r) would hit a negative argument and the program would be unusable at that point (NO_APPLIED_UPDATE, z unchanged). The current targets (tau=1/64, k values bounded) keep r well within [-1, 0.0625], so this is not an immediate risk but should be monitored. (4) The archived parent L00_s1_W2 finite-root expression remains valid for its original finite-task scope; this smooth body is the field-stage operator and does not re-grade that result. (5) C=0 is the source-published choice; a nonzero C could in principle reduce the O(ur) cross-term but would couple the slots and is outside the R1 construction. Retain unless successor feedback shows the cross-term dominates the stability budget."}